From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] wificond: look for wlan0 in sysfs before asking for its index

While Wi-Fi goes on or off, wificond checks every 50 ms whether wlan0 is there
(0001). It asked with if_nametoindex(), i.e. SIOCGIFINDEX, and for a name that
does not exist the kernel's dev_ioctl() first calls dev_load(), which, as
wificond has CAP_NET_ADMIN, asks for a module "netdev-wlan0". SELinux refuses
that (wificond may not request modules) and logs it: about twenty denials
each time Wi-Fi went on or off on the device (Test V40.1).

/sys/class/net/wlan0 answers without that: register_netdevice() adds it before
list_netdevice() makes the name known, and rollback_registered_many() removes
it after unlist_netdevice(). Without the entry there is no wlan0; with it,
SIOCGIFINDEX decides as before, now for a name that exists. wificond already
reads under /sys/class/net/wlan0 (the driver link), so no new permission.

--- a/pw3_radio.cpp
+++ b/pw3_radio.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 namespace wificond {
 namespace {
 constexpr const char* kModule = "/sys/module/ath6kl_sdio";
+constexpr const char* kInterface = "/sys/class/net/wlan0";
 constexpr const char* kDriver = "/sys/class/net/wlan0/phy80211/device/driver";
 constexpr const char* kModuleProperty = "vendor.pw3.wifi.modules";
 constexpr const char* kExpectedDriver = "ath6kl_sdio";
@@ -30,6 +31,17 @@
 }
 
 bool ReadInterfaceState(bool* exists) {
+  // Absence from sysfs first: SIOCGIFINDEX for a name that does not exist makes
+  // the kernel ask for a module "netdev-wlan0" (dev_load()), which SELinux
+  // refuses and logs, at every 50 ms step of a wait. The entry is added before
+  // the name can be looked up and removed after, so without it there is no
+  // wlan0; with it, SIOCGIFINDEX still decides.
+  struct stat st {};
+  if (stat(kInterface, &st) != 0) {
+    if (errno != ENOENT) return false;
+    *exists = false;
+    return true;
+  }
   // AOSP 8.1 Bionic preserves socket/ioctl errno. On this kernel SIOCGIFINDEX
   // reports ENODEV for an absent name; other failures are not absence.
   errno = 0;
